WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.100
Can you repeat the question one more time?

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.400
Uhm...

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.100
I think...

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.940
I don't know... Gosh! Sorry.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:25.900
I remember...

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:29.100
Ooo, I feel bad calling my family out!

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.300
We were at the DMV once and my mom...

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.060
made a comment about...

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.800
“Well, you’re in America, you should speak English!” because someone was...

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.340
speaking to their family member... in...

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.000
I think a Middle Eastern language, and I corrected her.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.440
I was like, “America doesn’t have a national language, we’re a nation of immigrants.”

00:00:53.680 --> 00:01:00.360
Within, like, the Hispanic community a common comment that’s brought up is that they don’t want their...

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.100
daughters and sons to marry or date

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.800
Black people. A lot of the times there’s a sense of humor behind that; that it’s just a joke, or,

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:15.659
it’s harmless and it’s not serious. But to me, even if it is a joke, it’s not a joke that’s funny.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:22.400
Out of my entire family, I’m actually the only one who is in support of the Black Lives Matter movement and...

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.760
It is very difficult to talk to my family.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:31.880
One thing that I want to do is to get together and perhaps have like a movie night and

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:38.700
suggest for example, [the movie] 13th, that does explore the concept of systemic racism.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.120
Let the people know around you

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:42.700
the...

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.280
current effects of, like,

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:50.840
colonization, and the attitude that we’ve all gotten, kind of, from being taught through an entire lens of white supremacy.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.920
Everybody thinks of it as kind of a past thing.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.640
I remember the first...

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.340
slur when I was like 4 or 5.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:04.380
These guys were like, “Your skin looks like poop, you know, you smell like shit. We don’t wanna play with you.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:11.260
You’re the color of shit.” So it was kind of like, okay, I knew from very young that I was gonna encounter,

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:13.940
you know, these attitudes.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:21.480
I think there is internalized racism because racism is systematic. We get indoctrinated with it.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:27.060
So... I see a lot of colorism. Lighter, brighter, better.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.700
So the closer you are to whiteness whether it’s hair, whether it’s...

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:35.180
the way you speak, or... you know, I see—I see some of that.

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:38.220
Black people who are around people that...

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.220
seek to practice anti-blackness,

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.060
be you, unapologetically.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:49.740
The more you practice that, the more you—the more you... put yourself out there without any qualms or asking questions,

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:55.160
the more it forces those people to see you and to see themselves as a result.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:03:02.020
In Hispanic culture, we refer to some African Americans as “negritos” and that's a very... derogatory...

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:09.439
just... verbage that is common where we’re from, in our village where I came from and immigrated from. Being in this country as an immigrant,

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:17.000
and now luckily being able to be a DACA recipient and a Dreamer, the conversation I talked to my parents about is, you know, I...

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:23.220
“You have to have a conversation with me about police, because what that means for me, being deported possibly, one day,

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:30.020
but African American kids have to have this conversation about their skin color, which they had no decision of.”

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:37.400
Listen to Black people when we talk about anything that makes us uncomfortable. If someone in your inner circle is

00:03:37.769 --> 00:03:39.769
saying the n-word and not—

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:44.569
thinking you’re overreacting, or thinking we’re overreacting for being offended by it,

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:48.140
don’t let it slide just because that person is your friend.

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:55.420
My high school was predominantly white people, and white people saying the n-word was very common, and

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:59.060
no teacher or adult really questioned it.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:04.860
When addressing someone who is race—racist is to address them...

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.880
as hard as it is, with a little bit more compassion and kindness.

00:04:09.629 --> 00:04:11.629
I think when you...

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:13.739
address someone...

00:04:13.739 --> 00:04:18.709
boldly, and attack them, then they’re just on defense mode and not willing to listen. Kind of approaching it on

00:04:19.109 --> 00:04:21.929
more of a level of love helps people to be a little bit more open-minded.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.720
That’s hard too.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:32.060
Man!

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:36.680
If you have to explain that what you’re saying isn’t a race joke, maybe you shouldn't be saying it.

00:04:39.699 --> 00:04:41.680
Yeah, so actually...

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:49.229
It was toward someone, a person of color, and they mentioned that they had to be in the back of the...

00:04:50.259 --> 00:04:54.239
restaurant because of their color. And I did mention it to a manager,

00:04:54.729 --> 00:04:58.589
actually, about how I did feel uncomfortable about certain things being said, and...

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:04.410
It actually... kind of felt like it was being brushed off, and actually led me to leave the company.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.900
Being paid less than their white counterparts,

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.000
I know it’s very...

00:05:10.419 --> 00:05:13.829
The rule that they tell you in the workplace is not to discuss

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:16.440
wages. I think... if

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:19.109
people can push for more transparent

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:22.599
conversations to have in the workplace,

00:05:22.599 --> 00:05:27.779
that’s a good way to be an ally and make sure everyone is being treated and compensated fairly.

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:34.470
So I think there’s a lot of training as far as sexual harassment goes and [dis]orderly misconduct within the workforce,

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:38.010
but I don't think there’s a lot of training, videos, that touch base on...

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.420
just... on racism.

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:48.000
So, they had a full day worth of training, and in this training... someone opened up in that conversation. And she was saying how

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:54.080
she’s a white woman who has a son who’s a white male, a teenager, and

00:05:54.280 --> 00:06:01.880
she has recently been so fearful of how he’s interpreting everything, and the way that he might be...

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:07.829
exposed to a group of people who have a mindset that, you know,

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:14.190
Black lives don’t matter. It got her so emotional that she was in tears, because she was asking for help. “How do I

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:18.820
educate my son to make sure he understands this?” and...

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:22.480
just by having that conversation, you know, it opened up a lot of doors.

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:28.260
I am in the holistic field, so I teach yoga, meditation, breathwork; and...

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:31.000
in this field you see a lot of

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:37.280
advertisements. You see a lot of the teachers who are prominent with large followings. They’re—they’re white.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:45.500
I feel that if we’re going to share culture, that there should be an amplification of Black voices just because they’ve been

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:47.409
actively suppressed.

00:06:47.409 --> 00:06:48.939
It’s seen as

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:55.500
unprofessional to have braids or to have twists and those are protective styles of Black women and men.

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:59.920
Black women are three to four times more likely than white women to die in childbirth,

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:06.940
and that’s because there’s these stereotypes that Black women are strong and aggressive, compared to white women being fragile and feminine.

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:13.880
And so it really has an impact on how Black people express their pains and how it’s received by health care professionals.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:25.080
It is important to listen, and you want to listen with the intent to understand. A lot of times people listen with the intent to

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:28.380
reply to everything, and we just want to be understood.

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:37.320
We have to put defenses down and stop assuming that racism is a problem for other people, but you yourself can never be racist.

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:41.700
I think that’s the most harmful idea to have.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:45.000
An easy thing that every individual can do is to vote,

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:52.240
you know, on your local level, your local government, for the presidential election.

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:55.520
I seek to make people that feel

00:07:55.840 --> 00:08:00.629
anti-Blackness, that are—that are struck by the sight of it, or the existence of it,

00:08:00.629 --> 00:08:03.989
I seek to make those people uncomfortable, because that uncomfortability

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:08.840
ultimately leads to them asking the important questions.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:18.180
There was a certain point that I... just do not... I was not attra—was not sexually attracted to, like, Black folks, like, Black men,

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:22.780
and I think it’s because of the implicit bias that I had growing up.

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:28.980
I try to recognize, within myself, in certain situations, and I try to fight against it.

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:36.140
If you want to see, "Is police brutality a problem?" You can just look it up. It’s right there.

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:41.860
America... oftentimes the real color that they see is green,

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:47.440
and if we can research where we put our money and see what they’re doing with it:

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:52.040
Who are they donating it to, are they using your money to oppress Black people?

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:56.889
Then you need to take your dollars and put it into companies that are more progressive.

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:04.809
I’m sorry. I thought I had so many good things to say.

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:08.210
Use...

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:13.400
whatever privilege you can to amplify Black voices.

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:16.720
They say I ‘talk white’, like, oh, “You’re the whitest Black girl I’ve ever met.”

00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:20.180
And I hate it. I’ve always hated it,

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:24.760
but in the moment, I’m like, hahaha, but it’s not okay.

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:30.320
Being brave. That sounds... probably silly, but I think it can be really, really hard to have

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:36.560
conversations like this and just taking the first step of, like, opening it up even when it’s really uncomfortable.

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:39.580
Anti-racism is...

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:43.119
not a one day thing. It is...

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:47.980
something that will take... the rest of our lives.

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:54.900
Even after all of the media has gone down, even after all of the protesters have gone away,

00:09:55.100 --> 00:10:00.520
even when there’s no one no longer saying it we still have to live it. We have to live it every day.

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:03.880
Don’t just say Black Lives Matter.

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:06.730
Live Black Lives Matter.

